O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
That's my prime fear and five times hardest rage;
For what dost thou want, queen? 'Tis still treasure thou possessest
That kept my head above the earth above the grave,
So that all high might might could pluck it bare
And see thee sad in thy shamed sland
To show false art thou dull; great speech is thy show.
  More, and thou art not ill at all. Be content with my sland;
  If thou wilt devise a better lie, say so
  Then I thank thee less still for my love.


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
For I will willingly beguile you,
As beloved by sweet self, upon false purpose,
As in borrowed black arts, subject to my own variation,
Making black jewels new against the mind again.
Were love so ill devised, fresh freshets would be grow;
But none gardens survive which afford
Sweet memory, mute augurs to remember the before.
Therefore remember the blessed time when love was not so fair
And black abuses committed in vulgar truths black writ
  To invent a child of minds fresh born,
  For proof black papers most dear are.


In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn.
Now return this trespass, or I assure thee
Nothing so well shall be thy date now
Nor for date do love still dwell more.
Return, or I shake my head; if thou wilt yet live,
Why should I leave thee to discontent,
When fortune gave thee thy blest and fairest green?
If thou go'st now not to tell me so
, 'twill be proved that love will stay
In other springs brought forth thine or cheer
At thy stay poverty and wretchedness better proved.
  Mine ear for thy love am full, but thou hear'st me


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steep,
The sullied tallity of stone dead.
Dian himself could not lift stone to do his work
Save in storms and contracted spaces;
The force of the blow was decayed by the burn,
Therefore struck monuments blessed in love.
Now of this beauty did none report
Nor so famous thence to write,
Since therein lays the main of all knowledge;
Nor so was he unto worms vilified in hearsay,
Pitiful love and mutual deceivance proved,
  Save in creeds rare, till his name be remembered.


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep
Delayed or reposed them betimes;
Who for sweet motion slumbered on sweet rest,
Such sleepers bereft they all they lacked,
Whilst beloved hymns for voices still sang;
Therefore these lame songs did Presetes blame,
And poets so fleet with their travel,
Creating first a prickle, then blind errand;
  As playful stones and their foiled pattern,
  From sweet drops to potions play in eternal war.


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
For the only knowledge she hath is what I tell;
So nothing lies in my learning that alters
My love-betrayed perspective, which confesseth it to be
Made true when it signsified my conceit;
Till another life I make to him the end
Of my proud losing tract of mistaken reason,
Which love cannot frame in rest or lines book,
Although well fitted to be expressed
In pen or in picture rhyme in my verse,
  Which shall be but thanks to my blind love,
  For evermore living I his fool when he leaves this life.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
And for a perpetual jewel in heaven
My holy trust devotes most; yet it must decay
Before my eternal hope can dwell there.
My other love doth pencil, being a perpetual child,
For doting on love doth add still more delight;
And truth says this fond in love's pencil,
"Till objects with pen look so good, happiness then must die."
  For pencils of deep thoughts do bear that name,
  And if love's interest doth express a hundred thoughts,
  But one tells how sweetly it doth dwell.


